I received a question about computing absolute free energies of hydration
from a BOSS user. Starting with BOSS 5.1, one should see test job FEPannihil.
The process has been fully automated. 

My response from 2008 follows.

Our first paper on computing absolute free energies of hydration by
disappearing the solute is Chem Phys 129, 193-200 (1989). We also 
developed the "double annihilation" procedure to compute absolute
free energies of binding for host/guest complexes in J Chem Phys 89, 3742-3746 (88).
J Comp Chem 14, 195-205 (93) describes computing absolute free energies
of hydration for substituted benzenes and deals with a system with torsional
degrees of freedom. 

For a rigid (note: rigid) molecule, one obtains the absolute free energy
of hydration by making the molecule disappear in water. This is done
by making the charges and L-J parameters go to zero for each atom; we also
reel in the bonds to ca. 0.1 A. In the BOSS Z-matrix file, the initial atom types
would be for the real atoms and the final atom types would all be 100
(q=sigma=epsilon=0). Under geometrical variations, each atom from #2 on  could be listed
with parameter 1 (bond length) and final value of 0.1 (this says to shrink all
bonds to 0.1 A). A series of simulations would be run perturbing from Rc (lambda) =
0.0 (real molecule) to 1.0 (all dummies); to avoid singularities, the last window
should be run, for example, as Rc0 = 0.90, Rc1 = 0.80 & Rc2 = 1.0, i.e. 0.80 <- 0.90 -> 1.0,
and not from 1.0 -> 0.90.

A flexible molecule is trickier and different cases may be handled in different
ways. Some examples are in the above J Comp Chem paper. In general, one has
to compute the free energy changes for making the molecule disappear both in the
gas phase and in water. This may again be done by perturbing the charges and L-J
parameters to zero in both media and contracting the bonds, but leaving any
bond stretching, angle bending and torsional energy contributors on. In BOSS,
this would require defining some dummy atom types with the same AMBER types as
the real atoms, but zero for q, sigma & epsilon. Bill

